{% from 'ingressroute.yml.j2' import monitoring_route with context %}
---
{# monitoring_route: a LogQL client cannot follow Authelia redirects, so the ClientIP guard is the
   gate.
   The only caller here is probe.py on a host; in-cluster callers use the ClusterIP. #}
{{ monitoring_route(
    container_item.name,
    container_item.hostname | default(container_item.name),
    container_item.port,
    '/loki/api/v1/',
    extra_client_cidrs=k3s_cni0_gateways + k3s_flannel_node_ips
  )
}}
{# DECIDED 2026-08-24: was PathPrefix(`/`) with extra_client_cidrs=['10.42.0.0/16'].

   Those two together gave EVERY POD the entire Loki API on an `auth_enabled: false` instance
   (configmap.yaml.j2). Not a theoretical reach — verified live from a source matching only the
   pod-CIDR clause: /config returned 200 (full config disclosure), /metrics 200,
   /loki/api/v1/push 405 (handler reached, so a POST would ingest) and /loki/api/v1/delete 400
   (param validation, so the delete handler is routed and the compactor has retention enabled).
   That is read of every log line the fleet emits, forged entries to poison the hosts' verdict
   lines, and a delete path.

   The macro's contract says guard 2 is "only the endpoint the check reads".

   A NetworkPolicy cannot contain it. baseline-ingress admits `app=traefik` on all ports and the
   traefik policy's first ingress rule has no `from:`, so any pod may reach Traefik — and
   everything arriving through this route carries Traefik's identity, not the sender's.

   probe.py runs on a HOST, not in a pod, so it genuinely needs the edge. It reaches only
   /loki/api/v1/labels and /loki/api/v1/query_range (scripts/diagnostics/probe_lib/core.py:107,118),
   hence the prefix. Every in-cluster caller uses the ClusterIP.

   The four /32s are the nodes' cni0 gateway and flannel.1 addresses: host traffic to the VIP
   SNATs to one of them, so this is "the two cluster nodes" and nothing else. Neither list is
   netpol_baseline_node_cidrs, deliberately — that var is read by 16 workloads, and a future
   widening of it must not silently widen this route too.

   Which address a host process arrives as depends on where the traefik POD is: same node, the
   packet leaves via cni0 and carries that node's cni0 gateway; other node, it crosses the VXLAN
   overlay and carries the SENDING node's flannel.1 address. Traefik is pinned to daniel-box, so
   daniel-server arrives as 10.42.1.0, which the cni0 pair does not contain: without the
   flannel.1 list `probe.py loki-labels` from daniel-server returns `404 page not found`. Both
   lists, because probe.py runs from either node and traefik's pin can move.
   tests/k8s/edge/test_loki_read_route_admits_probe_py.py is the guard.

   Residual, accepted: /loki/api/v1/ still contains push and delete, because PathPrefix cannot
   exclude. Those are reachable only from the two nodes rather than from any pod. #}
